{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310844,"nonce":"jLod78uheqE33CNQ1dikwfdHZ8Y=","title":"LLVM tier was silently DEAD (from_triple broke on LLVM18 static link) — fixed by upgrading to LLVM 21.1. Plus array-index reads now inline ...","message":"**The LLVM tier had been SILENTLY DEAD; fixed 2026-06-23 by upgrading to LLVM 21.1.** On LLVM 18 / llvm-sys 180 (keg-only llvm@18, static link), `Target::from_triple` failed for EVERY triple — even bare `\"arm64\"` which exactly matches a registered target — returning an EMPTY error string, despite all 42 targets being registered (verified: `Target::get_first()` enumerated aarch64/arm64; a static-link registry split where registration and lookup hit different registries). The tiered backend caught the backend-construction failure and **silently fell back to Cranelift**, so the LLVM tier (and `rayzor aot` LLVM emit, and `run --llvm`) had been non-functional. Memory claims that \"LLVM tier is slower than Cranelift\" were measured via the `compiler` example harness in DEBUG (no static-strip) — the production release bin's LLVM was dead.\n\nDIAGNOSIS METHOD (darmie steered: \"stop guessing, dump data\"): enumerate `Target::get_first()..get_next()` + test `from_triple` on multiple triples right after `initialize_all` on the main thread. Wrong turns ruled out by data: NOT thread-ordering (fails on main thread), NOT InitializationConfig (default is all-true), NOT registration (42 registered), NOT triple format (bare \"arm64\" also fails), NOT a build version mismatch (llvm-sys consistently used llvm@18). It was version/linkage-specific to llvm-sys 180.\n\nFIX = match the sibling **Zyntax** toolchain (identical init pattern, works): `inkwell 0.5/llvm18-0 → 0.7.1/llvm21-1`, `llvm-sys 180 → 211`, `LLVM_SYS_180_PREFIX → LLVM_SYS_211_PREFIX = /opt/homebrew/opt/llvm` (homebrew default `llvm`, 21.1.2). inkwell 0.7 API migration (compiler/src/codegen/llvm_jit_backend.rs): `get_operand(..).left() → .value()` (Either→Operand enum), `try_as_basic_value().left() → .basic()` (Either→ValueKind enum), param types are `BasicMetadataTypeEnum` so compare via `BasicMetadataTypeEnum::from(actual_ty)`. Committed. 715 lib + core_types_e2e green; Cranelift (nue default) unaffected; from_triple fixed, JIT --llvm runs.\n\n**KNOWN REMAINING — the LLVM BACKEND CODEGEN is broadly broken under inkwell 0.7/LLVM 21 (separate project to resurrect).** Because from_triple always failed in release, `compile_module_bodies` (MIR→LLVM) was NEVER exercised in the release bin — it has accumulated latent bugs that LLVM 21's stricter verifier + ABI now surface. CONCRETE bugs found (diagnostics, both reverted — re-add when picking this up): (a) **`select` emitted with an i8 condition** — LLVM 21 requires i1: \"Invalid operands for select instruction! select i8 %cmp, float, float\" (caught by per-fn verify in `cargo run -p compiler --example test_llvm_simple`, fn SIMD4f_extract). (b) **`build_call` SIGSEGVs** on essentially EVERY call (trivial `Sys.println`/string-concat program crashes) — poisoned CallInst → `Value::setName`→`ValueSymbolTable::makeUniqueName`→memmove crash (addr 0x…cfeedfac). compile_direct_call:5920 `build_call(*llvm_func,\u0026arg_values,\"call\")` IS the correct inkwell-0.7 API (forwards to build_direct_call); the corruption is the function/value the builder inserts into → likely compile_function_body's inkwell-0.7 function/builder setup. Void-name (`build_call` name \"\" for void) was a red herring (didn't fix it). DIAGNOSTICS: RAYZOR_DEBUG_LLVM_CALL (print callee/args before build_call), RAYZOR_DUMP_LLVM_IR (only fires in finalize, AFTER the crash). To exercise the JIT LLVM path: wire run_file's `_llvm` param into `auto_upgrade_to_llvm` (it was ignored → `run --llvm` silently ran Cranelift) — 1-liner at ~main.rs:1804, REVERTED.\n\n\nRan an A/B sweep (native/Cranelift vs `--llvm`) over all 151 `compiler/tests/haxe/*.hx`: **147 PASS**, and the rest are KNOWN non-LLVM issues — `test_copy_clone_identity` ([[bugs_test_copy_clone_identity_flaky]], native SIGTRAP flaky), `test_exception` ([[bugs_test_exception_two_bugs]], stack-overflow, flaky on BOTH backends), `test_for_in`/`test_map_literal2` (map iteration order — same multiset, native varies run-to-run). Two real LLVM-codegen bugs found by the sweep and FIXED (uncommitted, llvm_jit_backend.rs):\n1. **Vector shift panic (3 tests: test_q4km_dot, test_q4km_qmatmul, test_simd16i8_bitops — the nue Q4 quant-kernel path).** `VectorBinOp` int branch did `rhs.into_vector_value()` and PANICKED: MIR `shl/shr/ushr` on a SIMD16i8 (`\u003c16 x i8\u003e`) carry a SCALAR i32 shift amount, but LLVM vector shifts need both operands to be same-typed vectors. FIX: if rhs isn't a vector, coerce it to the lane element int type (trunc/zext) and SPLAT it across all lanes (per-lane build_insert_element); ALSO added the missing `Shl`/`Shr`(arith=build_right_shift(..,true))/`Ushr`(logical=false) arms (the int branch only had Add/Sub/Mul/Div/And/Or/Xor → shifts hit `_ =\u003e \"Unsupported\"`). Now Q4 dot bit-exact + qmatmul maxRelErr ~7e-8 on `--llvm`, matching native.\n2. **store-width UB (test_iterator: `for (x in new Range(1,6)) sum+=x` summed 0 not 15).** A for-in accumulation produces an i64 (spurious sext widening) stored into the i32 `sum` alloca → `store i64` into a 4-byte alloca = UB the optimizer miscompiled (only manifested for the temporary-iterator loop; the variable-iterator loop got lucky). FIX in the Store handler: when storing an int WIDER than the target alloca's allocated int type (via `ptr_val.as_instruction()`→`get_allocated_type()`), truncate to the slot width. Narrower→wider left alone. Verified +4 passes, zero regressions on re-sweep. Sweep harness: /tmp/llvm_sweep.sh (native vs --llvm, classifies PASS/DIFF/LLVM_FAIL/SKIP_NATIVE).\n\n**`--wasm function[664]::__init__` bug — root-caused + Bug A FIXED, 2 deeper errors remain (uncommitted, wasm_backend.rs).** PRE-EXISTING (since backend bring-up 2026-03, NOT a regression; nue avoids these stdlib fns; surfaced now only because the wasm-opt subprocess validates loudly). The invalid stdlib fns (FPHelper._floatToI32/floatToI32, Int64Helper.fromFloat, Output.writeFloat) are compiled into EVERY module, so ALL `--wasm` fails at init. Three bug families:\n- **Bug A (FIXED, 5/7 validator errors):** the wasm backend promoted `op_ty` to F64 for INTEGER-ONLY ops when an operand was f64 (e.g. `x | Math.round(f)` — Math.round returns f64), then emitted `i32.or`/`i32.rem_s`/`i32.and` on f64 operands = invalid. FIX: gate the F64/F32 promotion to NON-bitwise ops in BOTH the producer-inference (`allocate_locals` BinOp arm ~2041) and the emission `op_ty` (~3465) — bitwise (And/Or/Xor/Shl/Shr/Ushr) stay integer, float operand truncated. PLUS float modulo (`Rem`/`FRem` with f64/f32, e.g. Int64Helper's `f % 1`): WASM has no float-rem, so expand to `a - floor(a/b)*b` (matches native instruction_lowering.rs:176). Emission is `\u0026self` (can't alloc_scratch), so reserve a scratch local pair per float type in `allocate_locals` (new field `float_rem_scratch: BTreeMap\u003cValType,(u32,u32)\u003e`) and use it at emission.\n- **Bug B (FIXED — diagnosis was wrong):** FPHelper.LN2's IrGlobal was LOST IN MODULE MERGE (collect_globals saw 0 globals but __init__ referenced IrGlobalId 0/1, created in another builder module during stdlib lowering); old `unwrap_or(0)` aliased global 0 = __stack_pointer. FIX (collect_globals ~1090): 2nd pass scans every fn's Store/LoadGlobal targets not yet mapped, registers each as a real wasm global (idx ≥1, type from register_types); hardened StoreGlobal (~3889) to never fall back to 0 + coerce value to the global's type.\n- **Bug C (FIXED — diagnosis was wrong, NOT a fixpoint issue; the inference IS already a fixpoint):** MIR emits `cast i32 to \u003c16 x i8\u003e` (scalar→vector, from SIMD16i8-backed Int64.shl) and the wasm Cast handler's `emit_type_coerce(I32,V128)` emitted NOTHING → i32 left on stack → local.set into v128 = invalid. FIX (Cast handler ~3707): scalar→V128 emits the matching lane *Splat (shape from to_ty), V128→scalar extracts lane 0; both coerce widths.\n\n**`--wasm` STATUS after A/B/C: modules VALID (0 validator errors / 75 corpus tests; main runs; exit codes correct — arith=14, loop=45, float-mod=17 ✓ the float-modulo fix works). TWO RESIDUAL --wasm bugs remain (separate, found via exit-code A/B):** (1) **stdout NOT wired** — Sys.println/trace produce no host output (nue uses host onToken callbacks, not Sys.println → pre-existing runner gap, NOT a regression; runner must forward haxe_trace→stdout). (2) **array for-in returns 0** — `for(v in arr) s+=v` over Array\u003cInt\u003e gives 0 on wasm (native=50); wasm array-iteration codegen bug, likely latent (exposed now --wasm validates; may interact w/ the array-GEP MIR change). So --wasm validates+runs but NOT yet usable for printing/array programs; nue path works via other mechanisms.\n\n**ARRAY-GEP AUTO-VEC PAYOFF — VERIFIED 2026-06-23.** Ran a float/int reduction + dot-product test (/tmp/vec.hx) on `--llvm` with RAYZOR_DUMP_LLVM_IR. Optimized `main`: **0 `haxe_array_get_ptr` calls** (the per-element FFI barrier is gone — `a[i]` is inline GEP+load), and the read loops AUTO-VECTORIZE: int reduction → `add \u003c4 x i32\u003e` (4-wide, unrolled ×4); float reductions (`sum+=a[i]`, `dot+=a[i]*b[i]`) → `llvm.vector.reduce.fadd.v2f64` over `\u003c2 x double\u003e` with `nnan ninf nsz` (apply_fast_math reassoc is what enables float-reduction vectorization). Output bit-identical to native. This is the ORIGINAL motivation for the LLVM tier delivered: Cranelift can't vectorize reductions (no addv); LLVM does. The remaining `--wasm` Bug B/C are the only open blockers from this arc.\n\n\n**The ENTIRE \"LLVM backend is broken under inkwell 0.7 / LLVM 21\" saga was a BUILD-SYSTEM SYMBOL COLLISION, not a codegen bug.** The rayzor BIN depends on `wasm-opt = \"0.116\"` (root Cargo.toml), which compiles **Binaryen** from source (`wasm-opt-sys` → `libwasm-opt-cc.a`). Binaryen **vendors LLVM's ADT and EXPORTS ~1890 `llvm::` symbols** under the SAME mangled names as the real LLVM 21 (llvm-sys 211) — including **`llvm::SmallVectorBase::grow_pod`**, `llvm::raw_ostream::write_*`, `llvm::Twine::toVector`, `llvm::StringRef::str`, `llvm::StringMap`, etc. With both libs in the same binary the linker collapses each duplicate to ONE definition, binding LLVM 21's code (Module::print, ValueSymbolTable, Mangler, every SmallVector/StringRef op) to **Binaryen's vendored-OLDER-LLVM implementations** (incompatible struct layout) → `grow_pod` computes a garbage capacity → the **6.17 GB allocation** Guard Malloc caught in `Module::print` → SIGSEGV. Same mechanism corrupts names (StringRef→Mach-O-header garbage), makeUniqueName, getMangledName — ALL of it.\n\n**PROOF / CONFIRMATION:** (1) Guard Malloc (`DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES=/usr/lib/libgmalloc.dylib`, NO rebuild needed — the macOS ASAN substitute; real ASAN is blocked by `inventory` static-init + cdylib on macOS) caught a 6,171,891,696-byte alloc in `Module::print`→`formatted_raw_ostream`→`operator new`. (2) A fresh-context EMPTY module (0 fns/0 globals) print SIGSEGVs in the rayzor BIN but prints fine in the `compiler` example `test_llvm_simple` (which does NOT link Binaryen) and in a standalone `asan_min` example. (3) An `llvm_empty_print_selftest()` at the VERY START of `main()` (before Cli::parse, before any compile) ALREADY crashes → corruption is from STARTUP, not codegen/Cranelift/frontend. (4) `nm libwasm-opt-cc.a | c++filt` shows the 1890 `llvm::` exports incl. grow_pod. (5) **DEFINITIVE: commenting out `wasm-opt` in Cargo.toml + stubbing `wasm_opt_bytes` (src/wasm_cmd.rs) to a passthrough → rebuild → BOTH self-tests print OK, `T2.hx --llvm` prints `R=14`, `Tnostr.hx --llvm` exits 14. The LLVM JIT tier WORKS END-TO-END.**\n\n**THE FIX — LANDED 2026-06-23 (subprocess, then localized into our own distribution):** dropped the bundled `wasm-opt` crate from the `rayzor` bin (it can NEVER be a `rayzor` dep or a `[[bin]]` in the root crate — that re-links Binaryen → collision). Instead created a NEW workspace-member crate **`wasm-opt-helper/`** (package `rayzor-wasm-opt-helper`, binary `rayzor-wasm-opt`) that depends on `wasm-opt = \"0.116\"` and links Binaryen as a SEPARATE binary/process. `src/wasm_cmd.rs`: new `find_wasm_opt()` resolves `RAYZOR_WASM_OPT_BIN` → `rayzor-wasm-opt` sibling of `current_exe()` (dev `target/release/`, installed bin dir, `cargo install` `~/.cargo/bin` all co-locate) → `rayzor-wasm-opt`/`wasm-opt` on PATH; `wasm_opt_bytes` spawns it (positional `\u003cin\u003e \u003cout\u003e` for our helper which hardcodes `-O3 -all`; legacy flag form `-O3 -all \u003cin\u003e -o \u003cout\u003e` for a `wasm-opt*`-named program). Graceful fallback to unoptimized via `maybe_wasm_opt`. **So rayzor ships its OWN wasm-opt — no external binaryen install needed.** `.github/workflows/packages.yml` builds the helper + bundles both binaries into `rayzor-macos-aarch64.tar.gz` (unpack side-by-side). GOTCHA: cargo forbids `lto` in a per-`package` profile override, so the helper inherits workspace `lto=\"thin\"` (wasted but harmless; Binaryen object cache makes rebuilds ~1min). VERIFIED: rayzor has 0 Binaryen symbols (nm); helper optimizes gpu wasm 1.38MB→525KB exit 0 with relaxed-simd preserved (relaxed_dot survives); `run --wasm` spawns the bundled sibling (logged path) + falls back gracefully on the invalid trivial-program module; `RAYZOR_WASM_OPT_BIN` override honored; `--llvm` still `R=14`/`sum=45 OK`; whole-workspace + llvm-backend builds green. Binaryen no longer linked into rayzor → collision gone. VERIFIED: `T2.hx --llvm`→`R=14`, `T5.hx --llvm` (for-loop+ternary+string concat)→`sum=45 OK`, `Tnostr --llvm`→exit 14; default (no-llvm/Cranelift) AND llvm-backend builds both green; native path unaffected. The 3 real codegen fixes (discard-value-names, type-eq, select-i1) kept; all investigation probes cleaned out. **CAVEATS:** (1) needs `wasm-opt` (binaryen) on PATH for wasm optimization — else wasm runs unoptimized. (2) `--wasm` on trivial programs still fails at wasmtime `function[664]::__init__` — a SEPARATE pre-existing wasm-backend codegen bug (`haxe.Int64Helper.fromFloat`/`Output.writeFloat` emit `f64` where `i32` expected); the raw module is invalid regardless of wasm-opt (NOT caused by this fix). (3) the LLVM tier works for simple/moderate programs but still has OTHER codegen bugs for SIMD/complex code (e.g. an `into_vector_value()` panic on scalar i32 operands at llvm_jit_backend ~3728, hit via SIMD4f in test_llvm_simple) — resurrection is no longer blocked, but not 100% complete. NEXT: verify the array-GEP vectorization payoff now that --llvm runs (RAYZOR_DUMP_LLVM_IR), then fix remaining SIMD codegen bugs.\n\n**ORIGINAL FIX OPTIONS (for reference, superseded by the subprocess choice above):** keep wasm-opt's functionality while killing the collision. Options: (A) build Binaryen with hidden visibility / localize ONLY its `llvm::*` symbols in the archive (post-process libwasm-opt-cc.a with a build step — keeps bundled crate, no external dep, but must NOT hide the cxx-bridge FFI symbols). (B) macOS `ld -load_hidden` on the Binaryen archives (hides ALL its symbols incl. FFI → would break the bridge unless only llvm:: localized). (C) drop the bundled crate, shell out to an external `wasm-opt` binary subprocess (simplest+robust, reverses the \"no external bin dep\" choice in wasm_cmd.rs — but aligns with [[feedback_leverage_standard_tooling]]). (D) separate the wasm-opt path into its own helper binary. Current repo state: wasm-opt TEMP-disabled (stubbed passthrough → wasm output unoptimized but LLVM tier works); revert + apply chosen fix.\n\n**Real codegen fixes that ALSO landed this session (independent of the collision, keep them):** (1) `LLVMContextSetDiscardValueNames(ctx,1)` in with_opt_level — avoids ValueSymbolTable::makeUniqueName entirely (also a standard release mem opt); was masking, not fixing, but harmless+good. (2) declare_function type compare `format!(\"{:?}\",e)==format!(\"{:?}\",a)` → `e == a` (the Debug impl calls LLVMPrintTypeToString which the collision corrupted; direct PartialEq is correct regardless). (3) **`select` with i8 condition → narrow to i1 via `!=0`** (LLVMJitBackend Select arms ~2456 \u0026 ~3300) — REAL codegen bug LLVM 21's verifier flagged (\"Invalid operands for select instruction! select i8\") in SIMD4f_extract; keep. Run-`--llvm` wiring (run_file `_llvm`→auto_upgrade, main.rs ~1809) also landed. Many env-gated debug probes added (RAYZOR_FN_PROBE, RAYZOR_DECLARE_LIMIT, RAYZOR_COMPILE_LIMIT, RAYZOR_PRINT_TEST/PTEST_NAMES, RAYZOR_NAME_AUDIT, RAYZOR_LLVM_SELFTEST, RAYZOR_MAP_PROBE, RAYZOR_NO_GLOBAL_MAP) — clean up before final commit. `compiler/examples/asan_min.rs` is a scratch isolation harness.\n\n---\n**SESSION-2 EARLIER NOTES (superseded by the root-cause find above; the \"pervasive corruption\" was the collision all along):**\n\n**SESSION-2 UPDATE (2026-06-23 PM) — sharpened diagnosis + 2 real fixes landed (uncommitted):** The prior \"main/entry-block SETUP is corrupt\" finding is **REFUTED** — it was the `get_parent` confound. A direct `llvm_func.verify(false)` probe (compile_function_body, no get_parent) prints cleanly and `main` compiles 100% of its instructions \"ok\". The build_call SIGSEGV is actually **`ValueSymbolTable::makeUniqueName`**, which only runs on a **value-name COLLISION** (2nd reuse of a name like \"cmp\"/\"load\"/\"cond_i1\" in one fn). main never collides (its calls are unnamed \"\", one unique \"str_literal\"), so main \"worked\"; the crash first shows in `haxe.ds.TreeNode::new` (always dragged in by stdlib) and in a trivial multi-compare `main`. **Zyntax reuses names heavily (\"load\"/\"call\"/\"add\") and works → makeUniqueName is NOT broken; rayzor corrupts its own ValueSymbolTable.** Crash addr is consistently `0x0100000cfeedfacf` = first 8 bytes of a Mach-O ARM64 header (magic FEEDFACF + cputype 0100000C) read as a u64 → a name's storage is a dangling/garbage pointer into a loaded image. **FIX 1 (landed, uncommitted): `LLVMContextSetDiscardValueNames(ctx,1)` in with_opt_level** (llvm-sys raw; inkwell exposes `Context::raw()`) — makes every local setName a no-op (no symtab insert → makeUniqueName never runs), preserves GlobalValue names for JIT symbol resolution; **eliminates the codegen makeUniqueName crash.** **FIX 2 (landed, uncommitted): declare_function signature compare was `format!(\"{:?}\",e)==format!(\"{:?}\",a)` on LLVM types → `LLVMPrintTypeToString` SIGBUSes under LLVM21 (the AOT \"rayzor-aot\"-thread crash); replaced with `e == a`** (inkwell type enums impl PartialEq over canonical LLVMTypeRef). **STILL BROKEN after both fixes:** the module rayzor builds is **pervasively memory-corrupt** — `Module::print`/`print_to_string` (AOT IR serialization, aot_compiler.rs:275, ESSENTIAL not debug), `ExecutionEngine::getMangledName` (finalize add_global_mapping fallback, JIT), all SIGSEGV reading corrupt names. **Reproduces on a trivial STRING-FREE program** (Sys.exit(2+3*4)) in BOTH the JIT/MCJIT path AND the AOT/object-file path → corruption is in MIR→LLVM **module-building of the always-included stdlib**, NOT MCJIT, NOT strings, NOT user code, NOT Cranelift (AOT does no Cranelift/execution). **darmie hint \"Zyntax avoids mcjit\" is correct** (Zyntax's llvm_jit_backend.rs abandoned `create_jit_execution_engine`/`add_global_mapping` for object-file+dlopen) and is the right FUTURE JIT architecture, but it will NOT fix this bug (rayzor's AOT is already MCJIT-free and still corrupts). NEXT = dedicated ASAN session to catch the corrupting WRITE (likely a bad ptr/len passed to an LLVM C-API in MIR→LLVM, or a Rust-side OOB/UAF), since symptom-chasing peeled 3 layers (makeUniqueName → getMangledName → Module::print) all rooted in the same corrupt name storage. Diagnostics added this session (env-gated, harmless): RAYZOR_FN_PROBE (print each fn name in compile_module_bodies), per-instruction `[codegen]` trace via RAYZOR_DEBUG_LLVM_CALL (now all-fns, prints + per-instr verify), RAYZOR_MAP_PROBE + RAYZOR_NO_GLOBAL_MAP (finalize add_global_mapping loop). `run --llvm` wired (run_file `_llvm`→auto_upgrade, main.rs ~1804).\n\n**LLVM CODEGEN ROOT (deep-dived 2026-06-23, NOT fixed — needs a dedicated ASAN session):** the `build_call` SIGSEGV is **systematic heap/memory corruption in `compile_function_body`, present BEFORE the first instruction**. Localized via RAYZOR_DEBUG_LLVM_CALL (printed callee/in_fn before each build_call) + verifying/printing the enclosing fn: `main` is corrupt at the very first call (`rayzor_push_call_frame`); `f.verify(true)` and `f.print_to_stderr()` BOTH crash on it (header prints, then SIGABRT 134 / SIGSEGV — LLVM 21 is assertions-on, so the corrupt traversal aborts). Verifying `main` even BEFORE the first call is built also crashes → corruption is in the function/entry-block SETUP, not an instruction. NOTE the enclosing fn was obtained via `builder.get_insert_block().get_parent()` — possible that get_parent itself returns garbage under inkwell 0.7 (confound); next session should track a `current_function: FunctionValue` field set in compile_function_body and verify THAT directly to separate \"main is corrupt\" from \"get_parent is buggy\". declare_function/declare_extern_function (translate_type→fn_type→add_function) look standard; entry block via `context.append_basic_block(llvm_func,\"entry\")` + `builder.position_at_end`. CONTEXT is `Box::leak(Context::create())` (valid, not a lifetime UAF). Zyntax (SAME inkwell 0.7.1/llvm-sys 211, SAME `build_call(*fn,\u0026args,\"call\")` pattern, llvm_backend.rs:3010) WORKS — so it's a rayzor value/builder/setup difference, not the API. The crash is NOT related to the array-GEP change (trivial `var x=2+3*4; Sys.println(\"R=\"+x)` with no arrays crashes identically). NEXT STEPS: (1) current_function field + direct verify to confirm; (2) build with `-Zsanitizer=address` (nightly) to find the corrupting write; (3) diff rayzor's value_map / arg-coercion / block setup against Zyntax's llvm_backend.rs. Two known concrete codegen bugs that LLVM 21's verifier surfaces once it gets that far: **select with i8 condition (needs i1)** (test_llvm_simple, SIMD4f_extract) and this build_call corruption. To drive the JIT LLVM path: wire run_file's `_llvm`→`auto_upgrade_to_llvm` (~main.rs:1804, 1-liner) + the RAYZOR_DEBUG_LLVM_CALL probe in compile_direct_call:5918. ALL REVERTED to keep main clean.\n\nOTHER REMAINING: `rayzor aot` LLVM object/asm SIGBUSes (138) — same codegen-is-broken root. C-AOT (`rayzor aot --emit c`) WORKS and is the opt-in path; default emit is `Executable` (LLVM). aot LLVM target init runs on a spawned large-stack thread — fine. **Decision pending: resurrecting the LLVM codegen is open-ended multi-bug work; the from_triple/construction fix + array-GEP are the banked wins.**\n\n**ARRAY-GEP (committed, separate):** array element READS `a[i]` (and per-element `for (x in a)`) now lower to inline `load(arr.ptr) + GEP(idx, i64 stride) + load` instead of an `haxe_array_get_ptr` FFI call — removes the per-element call barrier so loops can vectorize / hoist (the L1 \"stdlib call never inlined\" lever, at its most ubiquitous). HaxeArray is #[repr(C)]{ptr@0,len@8,cap@16,elem_size@24}, uniform 8-byte slots, so it's the same i64-slot GEP as object fields. OOB unchanged (old call returned null→loaded→crash, same as unchecked GEP). hir_to_mir lower_index_access. Writes still use haxe_array_set. MIR confirmed call→load+gep. Test test_core_types_e2e array_index expectation updated (reads no longer call runtime). The runtime call is `haxe_array_get_ptr` (null+bounds check + ptr+idx*elem_size); the inline version skips the bounds check (a branch would defeat vectorization; BCE only runs in AOT/pipeline, not JIT).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_llvm21_upgrade_and_array_gep` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}